<title>UK: Chernobyl closure plans hit snags - report.</title>
<headline>Chernobyl closure plans hit snags - report.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-02-08</dateline>
<text>
<p>Plans to close down the damaged Chernobyl nuclear reactor have hit funding snags, The Financial Times reported on Saturday.</p>
<p>The Group of Seven top leading countries had agreed to finance two new reactors in Ukraine to help replace Chernobyl, scene of the world's worst nuclear accident in 1986.</p>
<p>But the Financial Times said a report commissioned by the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development (EBRD) has concluded that the plan does not meet its finance criteria.</p>
<p>The paper said in a front page report that at stake for Ukraine was about $1.8 billion of G7 aid. Half would be for completing the two new reactors and half for building up Ukraine's non-nuclear capacity.</p>
<p>Viktor Parhomenko, energy sector director at the Kiev economics ministry, told the paper: &quot;If the EBRD refuses us the credits, we will look elsewhere for the investment capital.&quot;</p>
